sections.s2_technical:
The access modifier hierarchy from most restrictive to least restrictive:

```java
// ACCESS MODIFIER ORDER (narrow → wide):
// private → default (no keyword) → protected → public
//    ←───── CANNOT move this direction when overriding ─────
//    ─────→ CAN move this direction when overriding ────────→

// RULE: override can STAY SAME or move RIGHT (wider). Never LEFT (narrower).
```

Every valid and invalid combination:

```java
// Parent method: private → NOT INHERITED, cannot be overridden
class Account {
    private void audit() { }  // private — child doesn't inherit this
}
class SavingsAccount extends Account {
    // This is a NEW method, NOT an override:
    private void audit() { }  // coincidental same name — not an override!
    // @Override private void audit() { }  // COMPILE ERROR: "does not override"
}

// Parent method: default (package-private)
class Account {
    void processInternal() { }  // default access
}
class SavingsAccount extends Account {
    @Override void processInternal() { }           // ✅ same (default → default)
    // @Override protected void processInternal() { }  // ✅ wider (default → protected)
    // @Override public void processInternal() { }     // ✅ wider (default → public)
    // @Override private void processInternal() { }    // ❌ narrower! COMPILE ERROR
}

// Parent method: protected
class Account {
    protected void validate() { }
}
class SavingsAccount extends Account {
    @Override protected void validate() { }   // ✅ same
    @Override public void validate() { }       // ✅ wider (protected → public)
    // @Override void validate() { }           // ❌ narrower (protected → default)! COMPILE ERROR
    // @Override private void validate() { }   // ❌ narrower! COMPILE ERROR
}

// Parent method: public
class Account {
    public void deposit(BigDecimal amount) { }
}
class SavingsAccount extends Account {
    @Override public void deposit(BigDecimal amount) { }  // ✅ same (only option!)
    // @Override protected void deposit() { }  // ❌ narrower! COMPILE ERROR
    // @Override void deposit() { }            // ❌ narrower! COMPILE ERROR
    // @Override private void deposit() { }    // ❌ narrower! COMPILE ERROR
    // public is the WIDEST — can only stay public.
}
```

WHY widening is allowed but narrowing is not — Liskov Substitution Principle:

```java
// Code written against the parent:
public void processAccount(Account account) {
    account.validate();  // protected — accessible from same package or subclass
}

// If SavingsAccount narrows validate() to private:
// processAccount(new SavingsAccount(...));
// account.validate() → FAILS! Can't access private method through Account reference!
// The child BROKE code that worked with the parent. Liskov violated.

// If SavingsAccount widens validate() to public:
// account.validate() → WORKS! Public is accessible from everywhere.
// The child is MORE accessible — callers are HAPPY. Liskov satisfied.
```

STATIC methods — HIDDEN, NOT overridden:

```java
class Account {
    public static String getPolicy() { return "Standard Policy"; }
}
class SavingsAccount extends Account {
    public static String getPolicy() { return "Savings Policy"; }  // HIDING, not overriding!
}

// PROOF that it's hiding, not overriding:
Account ref = new SavingsAccount("SAV001", BigDecimal.ZERO);
System.out.println(ref.getPolicy());
// Output: "Standard Policy" — NOT "Savings Policy"!
// WHY: static methods are resolved at COMPILE TIME based on the VARIABLE TYPE (Account).
// If it were a true override: would call SavingsAccount.getPolicy() via dynamic dispatch.
// But static has NO dynamic dispatch. It binds to the class at compile time.
```

FINAL methods — SEALED:

```java
class Account {
    public final BigDecimal getBalance() { return balance; }
    // FINAL: no child can change this behavior. EVER.
    // WHY: getBalance() must ALWAYS return the real balance — security critical.
}
class SavingsAccount extends Account {
    // @Override public BigDecimal getBalance() { return BigDecimal.ZERO; }
    // ❌ COMPILE ERROR: "cannot override final method from Account"
}
```

